      "paragraph_sha256": "fce78eb744ac918705095853edd7aefb00c3ae78ae3a7e72e6a1d9d9a7fd3591",
      "paragraph_text": "Equivalently, differentiating $`e^{x\\ln x}`$ by the chain and product rules gives the same expression. The constant-power rule assumed the exponent's derivative was zero. Here the exponent is $`x`$, whose derivative is 1, and its variation creates the $`\\ln x`$ term. Substituting $`r=x`$ into a rule derived for fixed $`r`$ changes that assumption. A check only at $`x=1`$ would miss this mistake because $`\\ln1=0`$; at $`x=e`$ the correct derivative is $`2e^e`$, which distinguishes it from the incorrectly substituted constant-power result $`e^e`$. [Return to P5](lesson.md#p5) · [Hint P5](hints.md#h5)\n",
      "inline_math_delimiter": "$`...`$",
